        "title": "Randomized Control Trials in Marketing: Measuring True Incrementality",
        "excerpt":"   “Half the money I spend on advertising is wasted; the trouble is I don’t know which half.” — John Wanamaker (19th Century Retailer)    In digital advertising, this quote is optimistically outdated. With attribution models often claiming credit for users who would have converted anyway, it’s possible nearly all your ad spend is wasted.   To separate correlation (user saw ad and bought) from causation (user bought because they saw ad), we need the rigor of clinical trials applied to advertising: The Marketing RCT.      Related reading: This is the practical application of Instrumental Variables and Double Machine Learning.      Chapter I: The Fundamental Problem   Why can’t we just compare Conversion Rate (Saw Ad) vs Conversion Rate (No Ad)?   Because of Selection Bias. Algorithms target people likely to buy.     Group A (Saw Ad): People who love shoes.   Group B (No Ad): People who hate shoes.   If Group A buys more shoes, is it the ad, or did they just like shoes?   \\[E[Y | T=1] - E[Y | T=0] = \\underbrace{ATT}_{\\text{True Effect}} + \\underbrace{Bias}_{\\text{Selection}}\\]  To zero out the Bias, we must randomize.     Chapter II: The Placebo Ad (PSA) Approach   The “Classical” RCT approach:     Identify a target audience.   Randomize them 50/50.   Show Treatment Group the real ad (e.g., Nike).   Show Control Group a “Placebo” ad (e.g., Charity/Red Cross).   Pros: Perfect counterfactual. We know exactly who is in the control group (those who saw the charity ad). Cons:     Cost: You pay for the charity impressions.   Opportunity Cost: You waste inventory.   AdAuction Bias: The charity ad might win different auctions than the Nike ad (lower bid), so the populations aren’t exactly comparable.     Chapter III: Ghost Ads   Google and Facebook solved the cost problem with Ghost Ads.   The Mechanism     The ad platform holds an auction.   If your ad wins, the platform flips a coin before showing the ad.            Head (Treatment): Show your ad. Tag user “Saw Ad”.       Tails (Control): Suppress your ad. Show the next highest bidder’s ad. Log a “Ghost Impression”.           Crucially, we now have a control group of users who would have seen your ad, but didn’t. And we didn’t pay for a charity ad.   The Analysis   We compare the conversion rates of the Exposed vs Ghost Exposed.   \\[Lift = \\frac{CVR_{T} - CVR_{C}}{CVR_{C}}\\]  import numpy as np import scipy.stats as stats  def analyze_ghost_experiment(treatment_conv, treatment_n, control_conv, control_n):     \"\"\"     Analyzes a Ghost Ad experiment.      Args:         treatment_conv: Number of conversions in treatment         treatment_n: Total ghost/actual impressions in treatment         control_conv: Number of conversions in control         control_n: Total ghost impressions in control     \"\"\"     p_t = treatment_conv / treatment_n     p_c = control_conv / control_n      lift = (p_t - p_c) / p_c      # Z-test for proportions     p_pooled = (treatment_conv + control_conv) / (treatment_n + control_n)     se = np.sqrt(p_pooled * (1 - p_pooled) * (1/treatment_n + 1/control_n))     z_score = (p_t - p_c) / se     p_value = (1 - stats.norm.cdf(abs(z_score))) * 2      return lift, p_value  # Example # Treatment: 50,000 users saw ad, 500 converted (1%) # Control: 50,000 users logged ghost impression, 450 converted (0.9%) lift, p = analyze_ghost_experiment(500, 50000, 450, 50000) print(f\"Incremental Lift: {lift:.2%}\") print(f\"P-value: {p:.4f}\") # Incremental Lift: 11.11% # P-value: 0.13 (Not significant!)     Chapter IV: Intent-to-Treat (ITT)   Sometimes we can’t track “Ghost Impressions”. For example, in email marketing, we can randomize the list but we can’t force people to open the email.   We analyze based on assignment, not exposure.      ITT Effect: Effect of assigning someone to the ad group.   LATE (Local Average Treatment Effect): Effect on those who actually saw it.   \\[LATE = \\frac{ITT}{\\text{Compliance Rate}}\\]  If valid assignment increased sales by $10 (ITT), but only $50\\%$ of people opened the email (Compliance): \\(LATE = \\$10 / 0.5 = \\$20\\) Every opened email generated $20.     Chapter V: Geo-Experiments (GeoX)   When digital tracking fails (e.g., TV ads, privacy changes, cookies dying), we return to geography.   The Design     Divide country into 100 markets (DMA - Designated Market Areas).   Build a Synthetic Control for your treatment regions.   Shut off ads in Control regions (“Dark/Holdout”).   Blast ads in Treatment regions.   Time-Based Regression (TBR)  We predict what the revenue in Treatment regions would have been using the Control regions.   \\[Y_{treat}(t) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 Y_{control}(t) + \\epsilon\\]  Train on pre-test period. Predict for test period. The difference is lift.   import pandas as pd from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression  class GeoLift:     def __init__(self, df, treat_geo, control_geos, date_col, metric_col):         self.data = df         self.treat = treat_geo         self.controls = control_geos         self.model = LinearRegression()      def fit_predict(self, test_start_date):         # Pre-test data         pre_data = self.data[self.data['date'] &lt; test_start_date]          X_pre = pre_data[self.controls].sum(axis=1).values.reshape(-1, 1)         y_pre = pre_data[self.treat].values          self.model.fit(X_pre, y_pre)         r2 = self.model.score(X_pre, y_pre)          # Test period         test_data = self.data[self.data['date'] &gt;= test_start_date]         X_test = test_data[self.controls].sum(axis=1).values.reshape(-1, 1)         y_actual = test_data[self.treat].values          y_pred = self.model.predict(X_test)          incremental = (y_actual - y_pred).sum()         lift = incremental / y_pred.sum()          return lift, r2  # Note: In production, use Facebook's GeoLift R package or Google's CausalImpact     Chapter VI: The Hierarchy of Evidence   Not all “lift” numbers are created equal.      Ghost Ads / PSA RCTs: Gold standard. User-level precision.   Geo-Experiments: Silver standard. Robust to cookie loss, but high variance.   Observational Methods (matching): Bronze. Good for directional checks.   Last-Touch Attribution: Garbage. Ignores causality completely.   When to use what?     Retargeting: Must use RCT (Ghost Ads). Selection bias is huge here (retargeting users who just visited cart).   Brand Awareness (TV/Youtube): Geo-Experiments.   Search Ads: Incrementality is usually low (cannibalization of organic SEO). Test via GeoX or Paid Search Holdouts.     Summary   In modern marketing, “Data-Driven” isn’t enough. You must be “Evidence-Based”. Data says the ad drove sales (correlation). Evidence says the ad caused sales (causality).   Without RCTs, you are likely paying rent for customers you already own.      “If you cannot measure it, you cannot improve it.” — Lord Kelvin “If you measure it wrong, you will improve the wrong thing.” — Modern Corollary   ","categories": ["Artificial Intelligence","Machine Learning"],
        "tags": ["ad-tech","incrementality","randomized-control-trials","causal-inference"],
        "url": "/posts/randomized-control-trials-in-marketing/",
        "teaser": null
