        "title": "Incrementality Testing at Scale: Building Always-On Measurement Platforms",
        "excerpt":"   “Attribution tells you who got the credit. Incrementality tells you who did the work.”    Running one experiment is easy. Running a thousand is hard. In Randomized Control Trials, we established how to measure the causal impact of a single campaign. But a typical growth team runs hundreds of campaigns across Google, Facebook, TikTok, and TV.   This post covers the engineering and data science challenge of Scaling Incrementality: How to build a platform that automatically measures valid lift across every channel, 24/7.      Related reading: Builds on AB Testing Designs and feeds into Ad Tech Metrics.      Chapter I: The Scaling Challenge   Ad-hoc studies don’t scale because:     Contamination: If the Facebook team holds out 10% of users, and the Email team holds out 10%, some users are in both holdouts, some in neither, some in one. Interaction effects become a nightmare.   Opportunity Cost: Holding out 10% of your audience permanently means throwing away 10% of potential revenue (if ads work).   Manual Overhead: Setting up a Ghost Ad study requires custom tracking pixels and manual analysis.   We need a Universal Control Group (UCG).     Chapter II: Universal Control Group Architecture   Instead of per-campaign controls, we hash User IDs to create a global holdout.   The Modulo Strategy  Every user is assigned a bucket: bucket = hash(user_id) % 100      Bucket 0-89: Global Treatment (Eligible for everything)   Bucket 90-94: Dark Control (No ads ever. Use for long-term baseline).   Bucket 95-99: Rotational Control (Hold out from specific channels).   Infrastructure Integration  Values must be propagated to every ad partner:     Google: Upload Customer Match list with exclude on Control IDs.   Facebook: Custom Audience exclusion.   Internal: If you serve ads, check user_bucket at request time.   Key constraint: Advertising platforms eventually “forget” excluded users or match rates fluctuate. The UCG requires constant monitoring of SRS (Sample Ratio Mismatch) to ensure the control group remains representative.     Chapter III: From Multi-Touch Attribution (MTA) to Calibration   MTA models (Shapley, Markov) are granular but biased. Incrementality (RCT) is unbiased but sparse. Calibration combines them.   \\[iROAS_{channel} = \\underbrace{ROAS_{MTA}}_{\\text{Always on}} \\times \\underbrace{\\beta_{calibration}}_{\\text{From RCT}}\\]  If MTA says Facebook ROAS is $4.0$, but a quarterly Geo-Lift test shows actual lift is $2.0$: \\(\\beta_{FB} = 0.5\\) We effectively “downweight” the biased attribution model using the ground truth from experiments.     Chapter IV: Budget Optimization with Diminishing Returns   Once we have Incremental ROAS (iROAS), how do we spend the next dollar? We use the Marginal iROAS. Advertising follows a diminishing returns curve (saturation).   \\(Revenue = A \\times Spend^B\\) where $0 &lt; B &lt; 1$.   Optimization problem: \\(\\max \\sum A_i \\cdot x_i^{B_i} \\text{ subject to } \\sum x_i \\le Budget\\)   This is a convex optimization problem easily solved with Lagrange Multipliers (or scipy).   import numpy as np from scipy.optimize import minimize  class BudgetOptimizer:     def __init__(self, channels):         \"\"\"         channels: list of dicts with 'name', 'alpha', 'beta' (coeff, exponent)         Model: Revenue = alpha * spend ^ beta         \"\"\"         self.channels = channels      def objective(self, spends):         # We want to maximize Revenue, so minimize negative Revenue         total_revenue = 0         for i, spend in enumerate(spends):             c = self.channels[i]             total_revenue += c['alpha'] * (spend ** c['beta'])         return -total_revenue      def optimize(self, total_budget):         n = len(self.channels)         # Initial guess: equal split         initial_guess = [total_budget / n] * n          # Constraint: sum(spends) = total_budget         constraints = ({'type': 'eq', 'fun': lambda x: np.sum(x) - total_budget})          # Bounds: spend &gt;= 0         bounds = [(0, total_budget) for _ in range(n)]          result = minimize(             self.objective,             initial_guess,             method='SLSQP',             bounds=bounds,             constraints=constraints         )          return {             self.channels[i]['name']: round(spend, 2)             for i, spend in enumerate(result.x)         }  # Example Data from Lift Tests channels = [     {'name': 'Facebook', 'alpha': 50, 'beta': 0.6}, # High scale, diminishes fast     {'name': 'Google', 'alpha': 30, 'beta': 0.8},   # Efficient, diminishes slow     {'name': 'TikTok', 'alpha': 10, 'beta': 0.5}    # Experimental ]  optimizer = BudgetOptimizer(channels) allocation = optimizer.optimize(total_budget=10000)  print(\"Optimal Allocation:\", allocation) # Optimal Allocation: {'Facebook': 3788.12, 'Google': 5985.45, 'TikTok': 226.43}  Note how Google gets more budget because its $\\beta$ (0.8) indicates it scales better than Facebook (0.6).     Chapter V: Integrating Marketing Mix Modeling (MMM)   At the highest level (TV, Billboards), we can’t track users. We use Bayesian MMM (e.g., Robyn, LightweightMMM). This treats the whole marketing plan as a regression problem:   \\[Sales_t = \\text{Trend}_t + \\text{Seasonality}_t + \\sum \\text{Adstock}(Spend_{i,t}) + \\epsilon\\]  The Triangulation Strategy:     MTA: For daily, user-level tactical bidding.   Incrementality (RCTs): To calibrate the MTA.   MMM: To measure the “dark” channels and long-term baselines that RCTs miss.     Summary   Building an incrementality platform moves the marketing organization from “We think it works” to “We know what the next dollar will yield.”                  Level       Method       Frequency       Purpose                       Tacitcal       MTA / Last-Touch       Real-time       Bidding algorithms                 Strategic       Experimentation Platform       Monthly       Calibrating MTA weights                 Executive       MMM       Quarterly       Annual budgeting &amp; TV/Brand              “All models are wrong, but some are useful.” — George Box “Uncalibrated models are dangerous.” — Modern Data Scientist   ","categories": ["Artificial Intelligence","Machine Learning"],
        "tags": ["ad-tech","incrementality","experimentation","measurement"],
