        "title": "Deep Dive into Ad Systems: Retrieval, Ranking, and Auctions",
        "excerpt":"   “In the milliseconds it takes your page to load, a global auction happens. Millions of advertisers compete for your attention, and one winner emerges. This is the most sophisticated real-time market in human history.”    Ad Tech is often misunderstood as just “marketing.” In reality, it is high-frequency trading for attention. The engineering constraints are brutal:     Latency: You have &lt;100ms to respond.   Throughput: 1M+ queries per second (QPS).   Data: Petabytes of user logs per day.   This post dissects the architecture of a modern Ad Delivery System (like Google Ads or Meta Ads).      Related reading: This is the system that executes the Incremental ROAS strategy.      Chapter I: The Ecosystem Overview   When you visit a website (Publisher), the following happens:     SSP (Supply Side Platform): Represents the Publisher. “I have a slot available.”   Ad Exchange: The marketplace.   DSP (Demand Side Platform): Represents the Advertiser. “I want to buy users who like shoes.”   In Walled Gardens (Facebook/Google), these three are collapsed into one monolithic system.   sequenceDiagram     participant User     participant App as App/Website     participant AdServer     participant Retrieval     participant Ranking     participant Auction      User-&gt;&gt;App: Opens App     App-&gt;&gt;AdServer: Get Ad (User_ID, Context)     AdServer-&gt;&gt;Retrieval: Find Candidate Ads (1M+)     Retrieval-&gt;&gt;AdServer: Return Top 1000 Candidates     AdServer-&gt;&gt;Ranking: Predict pCTR &amp; pCVR     Ranking-&gt;&gt;AdServer: Return Top 10 with Scores     AdServer-&gt;&gt;Auction: Run VCG/GSP Auction     Auction-&gt;&gt;AdServer: Winner + Price     AdServer-&gt;&gt;App: Render Winner     App-&gt;&gt;User: Show Ad     Chapter II: The 4-Stage Funnel   We cannot score 100 million ads for every request. We use a Cascade Architecture.   1. Retrieval (Matching)  Goal: Reduce 100M ads → 1,000 candidates. Tech: Inverted Index (Elasticsearch/Lucene) or Vector Search (FAISS/ScaNN).   Query: “User likes Running AND Tech AND Loc=NYC” Returns: All active campaigns targeting these keys.   2. Light Ranking (Filtering)  Goal: Reduce 1,000 → 100. Logic: Hard constraints.     Budget exhausted?   Brand safety rules? (Don’t show gambling ads on kids’ content)   Frequency cap reached? (User saw this ad 5 times today)   3. Heavy Ranking (Scoring)  Goal: Score top 100. Model: Deep Neural Networks (DLRM). The core equation for eCPM (Expected Cost Per Mille):   \\[eCPM = Bid \\times pCTR \\times pCVR \\times 1000\\]     pCTR: Probability of Click (Logistic Regression / GBM)   pCVR: Probability of Conversion (Deep Learning)   4. The Auction  Goal: Pick 1 winner. Mechanism: Generalized Second Price (GSP) or VCG.     Chapter III: The VCG Auction   Why not just pay what you bid? (First-Price). Because advertisers would “shade” their bids (bid $1.01 if the next guy bids $1.00).   In Second-Price Auctions (Vickrey-Clarke-Groves), you pay the opportunity cost you imposed on others.   Scenario: 1 Slot.     Bidder A: Bids $10.   Bidder B: Bids $5.   Outcome:     A wins.   A pays $5.01 (just enough to beat B).   Benefit: Truthful bidding is the dominant strategy. You bid your true value, because bidding higher doesn’t increase your price (only win rate), and bidding lower risks losing a profitable impression.     Chapter IV: The Ranking Model (DLRM)   The heart of the system is the pCTR model. Meta open-sourced DLRM (Deep Learning Recommendation Model).   Architecture     Dense Features: Age, Time of Day (Feed into MLP).   Sparse Features: UserID, AdID, Keywords (Feed into Embedding Tables).   Interaction: Dot product of embeddings.   Top MLP: Sigmoid output for probability.   import torch import torch.nn as nn  class SimpleDLRM(nn.Module):     def __init__(self, num_users, num_items, embed_dim=16):         super().__init__()         # Embeddings for sparse features         self.user_emb = nn.Embedding(num_users, embed_dim)         self.item_emb = nn.Embedding(num_items, embed_dim)          # MLP for dense features interaction         self.fc = nn.Sequential(             nn.Linear(embed_dim * 2, 64),             nn.ReLU(),             nn.Linear(64, 1),             nn.Sigmoid()         )      def forward(self, user_ids, item_ids):         u = self.user_emb(user_ids)         i = self.item_emb(item_ids)          # Concat interaction         x = torch.cat([u, i], dim=1)         return self.fc(x)  # Millions of parameters, trained on billions of logs model = SimpleDLRM(num_users=100000, num_items=50000)   Challenge: Embeddings are huge (TB sizes). Ad systems require Model Parallelism (splitting embeddings across GPUs) rather than just Data Parallelism.     Chapter V: Pacing and Budgeting   If I have a $100/day budget, I shouldn’t spend it all at 8 AM. The Pacing Controller throttles the bid.   \\(Bid_{sharded} = Bid_{value} \\times \\psi(t)\\) where $\\psi(t) \\in [0, 1]$ is the pacing multiplier.   We use PID Controllers (Proportional-Integral-Derivative) to adjust $\\psi$ in real-time.     Spending too fast? Drop $\\psi$.   Spending too slow? Raise $\\psi$.   See Online Bid Optimization for the control theory details.     Summary   Ad systems are the Formula 1 of engineering. They combine:     Search Systems (Retrieval)   Recommender Systems (Ranking)   Game Theory (Auctions)   Control Theory (Pacing)   Distributed Systems (Low latency)   If you can build an ad server, you can build almost anything.      “Latency is the silent killer of revenue.”   ","categories": ["Artificial Intelligence","Machine Learning"],
        "tags": ["ad-tech","ranking","auctions","systems"],
        "url": "/posts/deep-dive-into-ad-systems/",
        "teaser": null
