        "title": "Ad Tech Metrics Explained: CTR, CVR, ROAS, and Incrementality",
        "excerpt":"   “In God we trust. All others must bring data.” — W. Edwards Deming “In Ad Tech we trust. All others must understand conversion lag.” — Anonymous    Ad Tech is notorious for its alphabet soup of acronyms. But these aren’t just jargon—they are the physics of the marketplace. Understanding eCPM is as vital to an ad engineer as $F=ma$ is to a physicist.   This post explains the key metrics, how they relate, and the statistical traps that ruin dashboards.      Related reading: These metrics feed the Online Bid Optimization controllers.      Chapter I: The Hierarchy of Metrics   Everything flows from the Auction to the Bank Account.   1. The Auction Currency: eCPM  effective Cost Per Mille (thousand impressions). This is the only thing the ad server cares about. It normalizes all bids (CPC, CPA, CPM) into a single revenue number for the publisher.   \\[eCPM = \\frac{Revenue}{Impressions} \\times 1000\\]  If Bidder A pays $1.00 per Click with $1\\%$ CTR: \\(eCPM = \\$1.00 \\times 0.01 \\times 1000 = \\$10.00\\)   2. The Efficiency Metrics (Advertiser View)     CPC (Cost Per Click): $\\frac{Spend}{Clicks}$. Measures “Traffic Cost”.   CPA (Cost Per Action): $\\frac{Spend}{Conversions}$. Measures “Acquisition Efficiency”.   ROAS (Return On Ad Spend): $\\frac{Revenue}{Spend}$. Measures “Business Value”.            Good for e-commerce (Sales).       Target: $4.0x$ (spend $1, make $4).           3. The Supply Metrics (Publisher View)     Fill Rate: $\\frac{Ads Shown}{Ad Requests}$. “Did we waste the opportunity?”   Viewability: % of ads actually seen by user (IAB standard: 50% pixels for 1s).     Chapter II: The Lagged Conversion Trap   Scenario: You launch a campaign on Monday.     Monday Spend: $1000.   Monday Revenue: $500.   Monday ROAS: $0.5x$ (Disaster!)   Tuesday: User who clicked Monday finally buys.     Monday Revenue (updated): $2000.   Monday ROAS: $2.0x$ (Success!)   Most conversions happen days after the click. This is Conversion Lag. If you optimize based on today’s reported ROAS, you will kill your best campaigns because they haven’t matured yet.   The Solution: Cohort Analysis  Always analyze based on Click Date, not Conversion Date.   import pandas as pd import numpy as np import matplotlib.pyplot as plt  def plot_cohort_roas(df):     \"\"\"     df has columns: click_date, conversion_date, spend, revenue     \"\"\"     # 1. Group Spend by Click Date     daily_spend = df.groupby('click_date')['spend'].sum()      # 2. Group Revenue by Click Date (Critical!)     # Even if conversion happened 7 days later, it belongs to the click cohort     daily_revenue = df.groupby('click_date')['revenue'].sum()      # 3. Calculate Mature ROAS     roas = daily_revenue / daily_spend      return roas  # Simulating Lag clicks = pd.date_range(start='2025-01-01', periods=10) spend = np.random.uniform(1000, 2000, 10) revenue_immediate = spend * 0.5 revenue_delayed = spend * 1.5 # Comes 3 days later  # If we looked at data on Day 1, we'd see 0.5 ROAS. # If we looked on Day 5, the first cohort would show 2.0 ROAS.     Chapter III: Attribution Windows   When does credit expire?     Click-Through Window: 7 days (Standard). If I buy 8 days after clicking, ad gets 0 credit.   View-Through Window: 1 day. If I saw the ad (didn’t click) and bought tomorrow.   Trap: “View-Through” is often 90% cannibalization. See Incrementality Testing at Scale for why View-Through ROAS is usually a lie.     Chapter IV: Simpson’s Paradox in Ad Tech   Aggregated data lies.                  Device       Spend       Revenue       ROAS                       Android       $100       $200       2.0                 iOS       $1000       $1000       1.0                 Total       $1100       $1200       1.09           If you optimize for “Total ROAS”, you might cut Android budget because it’s small, or cut iOS because it’s inefficient. But maybe Android users are just cheaper to acquire?   Lesson: Always slice metrics by Dimension (OS, Country, Placement) before making decisions.     Summary   Metrics guide the algorithms.     eCPM rules the auction.   ROAS rules the budget.   Lag rules the reality.      “If you torture the data long enough, it will confess.” — Ronald Coase    title: “Ad Tech Metrics Explained: CTR, CVR, ROAS, and Incrementality” date: 2025-10-01 categories:         Artificial Intelligence     Machine Learning tags:     ad-tech     metrics     incrementality            measurement           ","categories": ["Artificial Intelligence","Machine Learning"],
        "tags": ["ad-tech","metrics","incrementality","measurement"],
        "url": "/posts/ad-tech-metrics-explained/",
        "teaser": null
