        "title": "Agentic AI Resources: A 2026 Learning Path",
        "excerpt":"   “The best way to predict the future is to invent it. The second best way is to read the papers of those who are inventing it.”    We are in the Agentic primitives phase. The stack is not settled. There is no “React” or “Django” for agents yet—just a Cambrian explosion of libraries.   This post is a living curriculum for engineers who want to specialize in Agentic Engineering in 2026.     Chapter I: Foundational Papers (Must Read)   If you only read 5 papers, read these using a Deep Research Agent:      ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models (Yao et al., 2022)            Why: The “Hello World” of agent loops.           Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools (Schick et al., 2023)            Why: How to train models to emit API calls naturally.           Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior (Park et al., 2023)            Why: The “Stanford Town” paper. Demonstrates memory, reflection, and social dynamics.           Voyager: An Open-Ended Embodied Agent with Large Language Models (Wang et al., 2023)            Why: Shows how agents can write their own code skills to solve Minecraft.           Reflexion: Language Agents with Verbal Reinforcement Learning (Shinn et al., 2023)            Why: Self-correction loops. “Wait, I made a mistake, let me fix it.”             Chapter II: The Engineering Stack   1. Orchestration Frameworks     LangChain / LangGraph: The standard library. Flexible but verbose.   CrewAI: High-level, role-based. “You are a Researcher, you are a Writer.”   AutoGen (Microsoft): Multi-agent conversations. Great for code generation.   2. Memory &amp; State     MemGPT: OS-like memory management (Paging context).   Pinecone / Weaviate: Vector Databases for Long-term memory.   3. Evaluation     AgentBench: Comprehensive benchmark across environments (OS, Database, Knowledge).   GAIA: General AI Assistants benchmark. Hard questions that require reasoning + tools.     Chapter III: The Learning Path (2026 Edition)   How do you go from “Prompt Engineer” to “Agentic Engineer”?      Level 1: The Function Caller            Learn OpenAI function calling.       Build a bot that can query a SQL database.           Level 2: The Loop Builder            Implement ReAct from scratch (no frameworks).       Understand Thought -&gt; Action -&gt; Observation.           Level 3: The Tool Maker            Build custom tools.       Handle auth, error handling, and rate limits within tools.           Level 4: The Architect            Build a multi-agent system.       Solve the “Infinite Loop” problem (Agent gets stuck).       Implement “Human in the Loop” for critical decisions.             Chapter IV: Future Trends to Watch      On-Device Agents: Apple Intelligence / Android Gemini Nano. Agents running locally on phones.   Digital Workers: Agents as employees. “Hire an outbound sales agent.”   Interface-less Apps: Why building a GUI when the agent just calls the API?     Summary   The field is moving fast. The frameworks listed here might be dead in 6 months. But the Physics of Intelligence—Memory, Planning, Tool Use—will remain constant.      “Code is how we tell machines what to do. Agents are how we tell machines what we want.”   ","categories": ["Artificial Intelligence","Machine Learning"],
        "tags": ["agentic-ai","llm","resources","agents"],
        "url": "/posts/agentic-ai-resources/",
        "teaser": null
