        "title": "Causal Discovery: Algorithms for Learning Causal Structure from Data",
        "excerpt":"Can we discover causal relationships from data alone? Causal discovery algorithms attempt to learn the causal graph structure—which variables cause which—from observational or experimental data. This is among the most ambitious goals in machine learning.   Introduction and Motivation   Most causal inference assumes a known causal graph. But where does the graph come from? Domain experts provide structure, but this doesn’t scale: we can’t manually specify relationships among thousands of variables.   Causal discovery attempts to learn the graph from data. While impossible in complete generality (correlation doesn’t imply causation), under certain assumptions, we can make progress.   The Goal   Learn a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) $G$ where:     Nodes are observed variables   Edges represent direct causal effects   $X \\rightarrow Y$ means “X directly causes Y”   From this graph, we can:     Identify confounders   Determine adjustment sets   Answer causal queries   Key Assumptions   Causal Markov Condition   Every variable is independent of its non-descendants given its parents: \\(X \\perp \\text{NonDesc}(X) | \\text{Parents}(X)\\)   Faithfulness   All and only the independencies in the data are entailed by the graph. (No “accidental” cancellations.)   Causal Sufficiency   No unmeasured common causes. (Can be relaxed with some algorithms.)   Algorithm Families   Constraint-Based (PC, FCI)   Use conditional independence tests to learn skeleton, then orient edges.   PC Algorithm:     Start fully connected   Remove edge X—Y if independent given some subset   Orient v-structures: X → Z ← Y when X—Z—Y and X,Y not adjacent   Apply orientation rules   Score-Based (GES, CAM)   Search over DAG space, maximizing a score (BIC, BGe): \\(\\text{Score}(G) = \\log P(D|G) - \\text{complexity}(G)\\)   GES (Greedy Equivalence Search):     Forward phase: Add edges greedily   Backward phase: Remove edges greedily   Functional Causal Models   Assume specific functional form (linear, ANM), exploit asymmetries.   LiNGAM (Linear Non-Gaussian Acyclic Model): \\(X = BX + \\epsilon\\)   Non-Gaussianity of errors enables causal direction identification.   Practical Implementation   import numpy as np from scipy import stats from itertools import combinations  class PCAlgorithm:     \"\"\"Simplified PC algorithm for causal discovery\"\"\"      def __init__(self, alpha=0.05):         self.alpha = alpha      def conditional_independence_test(self, X, Y, Z, data):         \"\"\"Test X ⊥ Y | Z using partial correlation\"\"\"         if len(Z) == 0:             # Marginal correlation             r, p = stats.pearsonr(data[X], data[Y])             return p &gt; self.alpha          # Partial correlation via residuals         X_resid = self._residualize(data[X], data[list(Z)])         Y_resid = self._residualize(data[Y], data[list(Z)])         r, p = stats.pearsonr(X_resid, Y_resid)         return p &gt; self.alpha      def _residualize(self, y, X):         \"\"\"Get residuals of y ~ X\"\"\"         from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression         model = LinearRegression().fit(X, y)         return y - model.predict(X)      def learn_skeleton(self, data, variables):         \"\"\"Learn undirected skeleton\"\"\"         n = len(variables)         adjacency = {v: set(variables) - {v} for v in variables}         separating_sets = {}          depth = 0         while depth &lt; n - 1:             for (X, Y) in list(combinations(variables, 2)):                 if Y not in adjacency[X]:                     continue                  # Test conditioning sets of size 'depth'                 neighbors = adjacency[X] - {Y}                 if len(neighbors) &lt; depth:                     continue                  for Z in combinations(neighbors, depth):                     if self.conditional_independence_test(X, Y, set(Z), data):                         # Remove edge                         adjacency[X].discard(Y)                         adjacency[Y].discard(X)                         separating_sets[(X, Y)] = set(Z)                         separating_sets[(Y, X)] = set(Z)                         break              depth += 1          return adjacency, separating_sets      def orient_edges(self, adjacency, separating_sets, variables):         \"\"\"Orient v-structures and apply rules\"\"\"         directed = {v: {'parents': set(), 'children': set()}                    for v in variables}         undirected = {v: set(adjacency[v]) for v in variables}          # Find v-structures: X → Z ← Y where X,Y not adjacent         for Z in variables:             neighbors = list(undirected[Z])             for (X, Y) in combinations(neighbors, 2):                 if Y in adjacency[X]:  # X and Y adjacent                     continue                  sep_set = separating_sets.get((X, Y), set())                 if Z not in sep_set:  # Z is collider                     # Orient X → Z ← Y                     directed[Z]['parents'].add(X)                     directed[Z]['parents'].add(Y)                     directed[X]['children'].add(Z)                     directed[Y]['children'].add(Z)                     undirected[Z].discard(X)                     undirected[Z].discard(Y)                     undirected[X].discard(Z)                     undirected[Y].discard(Z)          return directed, undirected      def fit(self, data):         \"\"\"Full PC algorithm\"\"\"         variables = list(data.columns)          # Step 1: Learn skeleton         adjacency, sep_sets = self.learn_skeleton(data, variables)          # Step 2: Orient edges         directed, undirected = self.orient_edges(adjacency, sep_sets, variables)          return {             'directed': directed,             'undirected': undirected,             'adjacency': adjacency         }   def generate_linear_scm_data(n_samples=1000):     \"\"\"Generate data from known linear SCM\"\"\"     np.random.seed(42)      # True graph: X → Y → Z, X → Z     X = np.random.randn(n_samples)     Y = 0.8 * X + np.random.randn(n_samples) * 0.5     Z = 0.5 * X + 0.6 * Y + np.random.randn(n_samples) * 0.5     W = np.random.randn(n_samples)  # Independent      import pandas as pd     return pd.DataFrame({'X': X, 'Y': Y, 'Z': Z, 'W': W})   # Example data = generate_linear_scm_data() pc = PCAlgorithm(alpha=0.05) result = pc.fit(data)  print(\"Learned structure:\") print(\"Directed edges:\") for v, info in result['directed'].items():     for parent in info['parents']:         print(f\"  {parent} → {v}\")  print(\"\\nUndirected edges:\") for v, neighbors in result['undirected'].items():     for n in neighbors:         if v &lt; n:  # Print each edge once             print(f\"  {v} — {n}\")   Equivalence Classes   Many DAGs encode the same conditional independencies:   Markov Equivalence Class: DAGs with same skeleton and v-structures.   We can only learn up to the equivalence class from observational data. Interventional data can break ties.   Challenges   Finite Sample   Independence tests can fail with limited data. Use regularization, bootstrap.   Scaling   $O(n^k)$ for conditioning set size $k$. Modern variants use local search.   Latent Confounders   Violate causal sufficiency. FCI algorithm handles this but recovers less structure.   Nonlinear Relationships   Linear methods miss nonlinear dependencies. Kernel-based, neural approaches exist.   Summary   Causal discovery attempts to learn causal structure from data. Key takeaways:      Constraint-based methods use independence tests   Score-based methods search DAG space   Can only learn up to equivalence class from observational data   Further Reading:     Spirtes, Glymour &amp; Scheines, Causation, Prediction, and Search   Peters, Janzing &amp; Schölkopf, Elements of Causal Inference   Causal Discovery Toolbox (CDT) Python library  ","categories": ["Artificial Intelligence","Machine Learning"],
        "tags": ["causal-discovery","causal-inference","structure-learning","graphical-models"],
